WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.840
So much undergrads, really nice
to be here for the full reunion.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.620
And welcome everyone, at least virtually.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.610
I hope I get to see you in
person at the next one.

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:16.692
I'm delighted to be able to talk to
you today about what's happening in

00:00:16.692 --> 00:00:18.830
the FinTech payment space.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:22.220
There's kind of a revolution going
on as most of you probably know.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.550
And I want to get your feedback as well.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:31.711
So I'm going to leave a healthy amount
of time at the end for you to comment or

00:00:31.711 --> 00:00:37.670
add questions, or give me your
thoughts on where this space is going.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.267
It's actually very timely for me because
I'm going to be talking to the GSB Trust

00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:47.415
in the next couple of weeks about the same
topic and I'm sure that you could give me

00:00:47.415 --> 00:00:50.490
some hints about the sorts of
questions that they'll ask.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.950
I've been working on in this area for
the past years,

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.120
both on research and policy work.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:07.010
And this year, it seems to be all
consuming in terms of the policy work.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:12.959
I've been doing some work on stable coins,
on Central Bank digital currencies,

00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:16.506
and on China's Central Bank
digital currency.

00:01:16.506 --> 00:01:19.446
And I can talk about that project work but

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:23.740
I want to jump into
the substance of the topic.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:28.659
And maybe give everyone a sense of why
we're actually discussing this and

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:33.182
what's wrong with the current
payment system, why is everybody so

00:01:33.182 --> 00:01:35.180
excited about upgrading it.

00:01:37.625 --> 00:01:40.297
For those of you that
are working in FinTech,

00:01:40.297 --> 00:01:45.895
you probably know that the protagonists
in this story are always Alice and Bob.

00:01:45.895 --> 00:01:50.093
I think that goes back quite a few
years from the computer science

00:01:50.093 --> 00:01:52.435
literature in cryptocurrencies.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:59.851
Alice, in this situation, wants to pay
Bob, the baker, $8 for a loaf of bread.

00:01:59.851 --> 00:02:04.160
And the question is,
how is she going to pay Bob?

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.669
Now today I'm going to be talking
about general purpose payments and

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:14.587
not as much about the wholesale
interbank payment system,

00:02:14.587 --> 00:02:19.506
and how that's also going
through a significant upgrade,

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:25.560
particularly in security settlement and
cross border payments.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.986
But if you have questions in those areas,
let's discuss that because actually I'm

00:02:28.986 --> 00:02:33.360
quite passionate about improvements
that are happening in that area as well.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.304
I mean, for example,
the treasury market last year,

00:02:36.304 --> 00:02:40.720
could have used a substantial upgrade in
its payments and settlement system, and

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.630
instead we got a very significant
problem in that market.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.218
But again, today we're going to
focus on Alice and Bob.

00:02:48.218 --> 00:02:51.572
And most of you know that in
most of the world to Alice

00:02:51.572 --> 00:02:57.300
would typically hand over paper,
money or more frequently a credit card.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.763
The credit card could be in her
mobile phone wallet overlay,

00:03:01.763 --> 00:03:05.890
but it's still typically
a credit card based payment.

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.353
And it would eventually flow
through the bank payment rails,

00:03:11.353 --> 00:03:14.865
like almost all payments
in the current system.

00:03:14.865 --> 00:03:18.821
So Alice will send a message
using her credit card or

00:03:18.821 --> 00:03:22.600
a mobile phone app, a tap,
a swish or whatever.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.325
And her bank will take
$8 out of her account,

00:03:26.325 --> 00:03:31.750
send it through the bank payment rails,
which is pretty complicated, actually.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.590
And it'll get to Bob's bank in a day or
two, maybe three.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:39.123
But Bob instantly gets a message
saying the money is coming, so

00:03:39.123 --> 00:03:41.040
you can give Alice the loaf of bread.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:47.508
That experience is working pretty
well these days for Alice, but

00:03:47.508 --> 00:03:52.174
she doesn't really know what's going
on in the guts of the bank payment

00:03:52.174 --> 00:03:56.062
rails that's costing Bob so
much to take that payment, and

00:03:56.062 --> 00:04:00.530
it's causing many other costs and
delays that I want to talk about.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:07.070
So these payment rails
are illustrated in this schematic.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:10.320
So Alice could be
customer one of bank one.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.710
She's the blue dot at the top left.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.220
Her bank is bank one, b1.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:22.421
And Bob's bank is the green dot, b3, and
he's maybe customer seven of bank three,

00:04:22.421 --> 00:04:27.940
and the payment is a deduction
from Alice's account at bank one.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:34.094
And then bank one will credit probably
bank three through the central

00:04:34.094 --> 00:04:38.700
bank payment system,
which in the United States is Fedwire.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:42.810
And it can also go through
a private payment system and

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:46.016
be merged and then flow through Fedwire.

00:04:46.016 --> 00:04:49.227
As I said,
this tends to take a long time, and

00:04:49.227 --> 00:04:54.840
along these payment rails there
are various fees being extracted.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.418
If it's a credit card payment,
the interchange fees that Bob has to pay

00:04:59.418 --> 00:05:02.560
are actually quite severe
in the United States.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.646
I want to illustrate that in this diagram,

00:05:05.646 --> 00:05:10.720
which comes from the McKinsey Global
Payments Report from last year.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.640
That's a bit of a complicated diagram.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.290
I'm not going to go through
every element of it.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.430
But I want you to focus on the left stack.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:21.940
That's the North American payments stack.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:25.830
I got the data from this report and
we compiled it so that it's easier to see.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:33.440
And you can see that the payments
stack for US, the US payment system or

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.779
the North American payment system,
including Canada, is heavily on

00:05:37.779 --> 00:05:42.409
the consumer side, meaning consumers
are paying a lot in the United States

00:05:42.409 --> 00:05:47.460
relative to the rest of the world and
particularly a lot for credit cards.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.504
So, the interchange fee for the US is,
depending on what card you have,

00:05:52.504 --> 00:05:59.620
it's $2 to 250 per 100, which is huge,
actually, relative to the actual cost.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:04.380
Europe regulates interchange fees for
cards, and it's much smaller.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:07.300
It's about 10% of
the payment stack in Europe.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.840
Well, it's on average 15% for
the rest of the world.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:16.897
Not only is the US payments stack heavily
weighted towards consumer payments,

00:06:16.897 --> 00:06:22.330
meaning the profit margins are bigger
on the general retail side,

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:25.000
it's also just bigger in total dollars.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.054
So, you can see from the legend at
the top that US payment revenues,

00:06:30.054 --> 00:06:35.286
and that's basically profits gross
of overhead and other expenses,

00:06:35.286 --> 00:06:40.150
constitute about 2.3% of US GDP,
that's huge.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:44.704
Probably many of you didn't know if you're
not a payments expert, that it costs so

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:46.665
much to run the US payments system.

00:06:46.665 --> 00:06:49.680
By the way,
paper cash also costs an enormous amount.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.660
And that's not counted in this diagram.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.930
Paper cash is also a pretty
costly thing to do.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.144
The rest of the world, well, let's say
take Europe, Middle East, and Africa,

00:07:01.144 --> 00:07:02.102
that's 1.7%.

00:07:02.102 --> 00:07:05.630
So the US payment system
is a lot more expensive.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.630
And it's not really because
it's that much better.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:11.590
It's because there are relatively
high profit margins.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:17.268
So many have been calling for
an improvement.

00:07:17.268 --> 00:07:23.983
And that's gotten off to a head start
this year because of China's big news,

00:07:23.983 --> 00:07:29.254
its central bank digital currency and
the fact that China has

00:07:29.254 --> 00:07:34.424
already had extremely,
efficient mobile payment systems

00:07:34.424 --> 00:07:40.350
run by the two large FinTech firms,
Ant Group and Tencent.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:44.888
And Americans have been kind of
commenters, at least have been needling,

00:07:44.888 --> 00:07:48.561
the Fed and Congress and
the administration saying, gosh,

00:07:48.561 --> 00:07:52.268
if China can do this,
why can't the United States do this?

00:07:52.268 --> 00:07:55.400
And it's,
it's gotten into a bit of a space race.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:08:02.051
But let's go to more on the fundamentals
and talk about Financial inclusion,

00:08:02.051 --> 00:08:08.754
and I've taken these numbers from
the World Bank's Global Findex Database.

00:08:08.754 --> 00:08:13.604
And I plotted on the vertical axis
financial inclusion as percent of

00:08:13.604 --> 00:08:18.030
households that actually have
access to the payment system,

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:22.389
whether through digital means or
otherwise through banks.

00:08:22.389 --> 00:08:28.153
And you can see that in high income
economies it's typically over 90%,

00:08:28.153 --> 00:08:32.007
in developing economies more like 60-ish%.

00:08:32.007 --> 00:08:38.230
China is quite high, India is quite high,
Kenya is quite high.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:42.993
Those economies, China, India, and
Kenya, have had the benefit of very

00:08:42.993 --> 00:08:48.840
effective mobile payment systems relative
to the rest of the developing economy.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:55.270
And China's numbers, if you updated this
chart to today it's actually much better.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:59.869
Moreover, the digital payment system
in China is very advanced because of

00:08:59.869 --> 00:09:05.460
Alipay and WeChat Pay, the two private
services that I mentioned a moment ago.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:10.710
So for example, if you're in a city in
China, 95% of payments are being made,

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:15.579
of digital payments which are most of
them, are being made by one of those two

00:09:15.579 --> 00:09:20.250
services, so they have a virtual
duopoly on digital payments in China.

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:26.020
One of the big pushes in
the United States, in Congress and

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:30.101
in the administration,
is to change the US payment system so

00:09:30.101 --> 00:09:33.170
as to increase financial inclusion.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:36.503
There are about 5 to 7 million households

00:09:36.503 --> 00:09:40.954
in the United States that
don't have access to banking.

00:09:40.954 --> 00:09:46.008
And about twice that many that
are relatively weakly banked,

00:09:46.008 --> 00:09:52.243
meaning they have very costly or
inefficient access to the banking system.

00:09:52.243 --> 00:09:57.767
So they're using special expensive
paycheck cashing services,

00:09:57.767 --> 00:10:02.502
and remittance services that
are extremely expensive,

00:10:02.502 --> 00:10:07.150
and the United States wants
to do something about that.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:11.713
At the same time the United States
is dealing with the advent of

00:10:11.713 --> 00:10:15.330
cryptocurrencies, as you well know.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:20.523
And just recently the president's working
group is developing a report on what to

00:10:20.523 --> 00:10:25.940
do about stablecoins, which is the type of
cryptocurrency that's commonly used for

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:29.722
making payments, I'm going to
get to those in just a minute.

00:10:29.722 --> 00:10:36.334
So we're basically on the edge of a major
change in direction in the United States,

00:10:36.334 --> 00:10:40.216
and I'm going to talk about
the options at the end.

00:10:40.216 --> 00:10:45.777
But let me just mention briefly the one
option is to improve the existing

00:10:45.777 --> 00:10:51.154
bank payment rails,
make them more efficient, faster, cheaper,

00:10:51.154 --> 00:10:57.760
more widely available and accessible to
the under-banked part of the population.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:02.452
Another approach is to let
FinTech payment services,

00:11:02.452 --> 00:11:07.968
including stablecoins,
compete with banks to make payments.

00:11:07.968 --> 00:11:13.000
And then, a third option is to introduce
a US central bank digital currency.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:17.748
Now, all of those have a myriad of
design choices or there's a big menu for

00:11:17.748 --> 00:11:21.271
each of those, and
we won't get into all of it today but

00:11:21.271 --> 00:11:24.870
I want to engage upon
some of those issues.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:28.866
If you look at Vietnam,
that's a country that's ripe for

00:11:28.866 --> 00:11:33.272
leapfrogging all of these issues,
like Kenya did, with fast,

00:11:33.272 --> 00:11:39.162
efficient mobile phone based payments, so
I expect Vietnam to happen very quickly.

00:11:39.162 --> 00:11:44.542
India has the benefit of UPI and Aadhaar,
which in combination have made bank

00:11:44.542 --> 00:11:50.107
rail payments extremely accessible,
cheap, fast, and easy to use in India.

00:11:50.107 --> 00:11:52.928
Although that comes with the need for

00:11:52.928 --> 00:11:57.569
everyone to identify themselves,
and sometimes Americans

00:11:57.569 --> 00:12:02.580
don't like to be putting their
identities into a large data set.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.554
What about cross-border payments,

00:12:07.554 --> 00:12:11.074
well I'm not going to talk
about that at length today.

00:12:11.074 --> 00:12:16.014
But if Alice, who is customer one
at bank one needs to pay Bob in

00:12:16.014 --> 00:12:20.574
another country, let's say Canada,
or Argentina, or

00:12:20.574 --> 00:12:25.704
some other country, then Alice's
bank needs to find a bank that

00:12:25.704 --> 00:12:30.752
has correspondent banking
relationships in Bob's country.

00:12:30.752 --> 00:12:36.608
That would be bank three finding bank
four, times those food chains of finding

00:12:36.608 --> 00:12:42.563
a correspondent banking relationship
extend to three or four along the chain.

00:12:42.563 --> 00:12:47.498
And every bank along that chain needs to
check the modality of the payment and

00:12:47.498 --> 00:12:49.204
of course needs to check,

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:00.570
Well, to make sure that it gets paid for
the payment as well.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:04.491
So it's a very, very expensive and
annoyingly slow,

00:13:04.491 --> 00:13:09.265
and I'm sure you've all dealt with
that when you've tried to make

00:13:09.265 --> 00:13:13.381
a cross-border payment or
a remittance payment.

00:13:13.381 --> 00:13:19.520
If we go the bank railed route, we could
do what's called a fast payment system.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:25.471
So the key defining properties of that
are that it's available 24-7-365,

00:13:25.471 --> 00:13:30.943
you have near real-time access to
the funds by the recipient of the payment.

00:13:30.943 --> 00:13:34.010
And there are various design approaches.

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:37.180
You can either do it where the payment
is honored immediately but

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:39.705
the banks settle it up
later among themselves, and

00:13:39.705 --> 00:13:44.570
they merge their payments with each other,
that's called deferred net settlement.

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:47.756
Or you can have straight through
real time gross settlement,

00:13:47.756 --> 00:13:51.200
which seems to be the way that most
fast payment systems are going.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:56.307
The United States is doing one of those
right now, the Fed is introducing FedNow,

00:13:56.307 --> 00:14:00.910
which will speed up the bank rail
payments and make them more accessible.

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:03.708
Although, if you want to
get a payment service,

00:14:03.708 --> 00:14:06.521
you still need to go to
a bank to get that service.

00:14:06.521 --> 00:14:11.674
And one of the reasons I believe
that the US banking system is so

00:14:11.674 --> 00:14:16.435
inefficient at payments is
that banks don't really have

00:14:16.435 --> 00:14:20.897
an incentive to make it so
easy to move your money and so

00:14:20.897 --> 00:14:26.254
cheap that they will discourage
you from using their services,

00:14:26.254 --> 00:14:31.012
in a Walled Garden sense, and
of course pay their fees and

00:14:31.012 --> 00:14:34.615
keep your money on deposit in their banks.

00:14:34.615 --> 00:14:39.519
And as you may or may not know that US
bank deposit rates are extremely low

00:14:39.519 --> 00:14:42.580
relative to wholesale rates.

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:46.360
That's a not very well understood point,

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:51.400
that US bank deposits are very
non-competitive in terms

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:56.130
of paying you what they're getting for
their money.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:01.306
So simply doing a fast payment system
like FedNow is a big step forward,

00:15:01.306 --> 00:15:05.618
but in my view it needs to be
coupled by something that causes

00:15:05.618 --> 00:15:10.560
the banks to compete harder to
offer payments more competitively.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.684
So here are some examples
of fast payment systems.

00:15:13.684 --> 00:15:14.239
Korea was really one of
the first countries to do this.

00:15:14.239 --> 00:15:18.328
Bank of Mexico has SPEI,

00:15:18.328 --> 00:15:22.814
which is very successful.

00:15:22.814 --> 00:15:24.615
Sweden has a private system.

00:15:24.615 --> 00:15:29.890
United Kingdom has introduced
a nonprofit utility.

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:31.398
Singapore has fast payments,

00:15:31.398 --> 00:15:34.530
including cross border fast
payments that it's introducing.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:41.180
The European Central Bank has its
TARGET Instant Payment system or TIPS.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:43.842
And as I mentioned,
the US is coming online with FedNow,

00:15:43.842 --> 00:15:46.120
probably in the next year and
a half to two years.

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:48.584
So that's one approach.

00:15:48.584 --> 00:15:51.840
Another approach,
as I mentioned, is stablecoins.

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:56.900
And you can think Libra, but
now it's changed its name to Diem and

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:58.310
it's been reorganized.

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:04.343
Or you can think of coin bases,
stable coin, which is called USDC.

00:16:04.343 --> 00:16:06.540
These are the two largest stable coins.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:11.539
Well, Tether is the largest but
it's not as stable as,

00:16:11.539 --> 00:16:16.325
I don't include it as really stable,
although its price stays near $1.

00:16:16.325 --> 00:16:19.749
The assets backing
Tether are very sketchy.

00:16:19.749 --> 00:16:24.314
But here's how Alice would get her
stable coins to make payments in

00:16:24.314 --> 00:16:25.710
the United States.

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:30.770
She would go to her issuer,
which could be her bank, or

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:37.372
it could be the Facebook which will
provide it through its issuer of DM.

00:16:37.372 --> 00:16:40.574
Or it could be at coin-based,

00:16:40.574 --> 00:16:45.882
you'll get online and
load your wallet with USDC.

00:16:45.882 --> 00:16:51.443
And so in this case the issuer was taking
$500 in regular money from Alice and

00:16:51.443 --> 00:16:54.270
giving her 500 stable coins tokens.

00:16:55.930 --> 00:17:01.239
The issuer gives $500 to the trust that
owns the assets backing the stable coin,

00:17:01.239 --> 00:17:03.719
assuming there are assets backing it.

00:17:03.719 --> 00:17:07.751
And then the asset invests that
money in the backing assets,

00:17:07.751 --> 00:17:12.904
which can be bank deposits, they can
be treasury bills, commercial paper.

00:17:12.904 --> 00:17:18.429
In the case of Tether, the backing
assets were basically a black box until

00:17:18.429 --> 00:17:24.221
there was litigation in New York state
that forced them to disclose that it's

00:17:24.221 --> 00:17:30.306
mostly commercial paper and not as safe
perhaps as US authorities might prefer.

00:17:30.306 --> 00:17:35.228
The trust will then use those
assets to authorize the issuer

00:17:35.228 --> 00:17:37.599
to give the tokens to Alice.

00:17:37.599 --> 00:17:43.995
Now she can go out and
send them anywhere, using a mobile app or

00:17:43.995 --> 00:17:50.022
Internet online, and
make payments, or do peer to peer,

00:17:50.022 --> 00:17:55.680
pay for a cup of coffee,
whatever she wants.

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:59.181
Now the authorities in the United States
are quite concerned on a number of

00:17:59.181 --> 00:17:59.696
grounds.

00:17:59.696 --> 00:18:05.195
First, will the assets backing
these tokens be solid?

00:18:05.195 --> 00:18:12.732
Second, will all of the money coming
out of the banks cause a problem for

00:18:12.732 --> 00:18:16.889
the banks not having enough deposits.

00:18:16.889 --> 00:18:18.950
I'm actually not worried about that one.

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:25.751
Another issue is, will the blockchain on
which the tokens are issued be stable,

00:18:25.751 --> 00:18:29.762
operationally stable and up 24/7 365?

00:18:29.762 --> 00:18:32.003
If a lot of Americans start
depending on stable coins,

00:18:32.003 --> 00:18:33.720
the authorities are worried about that.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.503
So those are on the worry side.

00:18:36.503 --> 00:18:41.159
On the positive side, the advent of
stable coins may provide a lot of new

00:18:41.159 --> 00:18:46.281
opportunities to make new kinds of
payments, like doing Internet of things,

00:18:46.281 --> 00:18:50.350
smart contracting, programmable payments.

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:53.739
And they may also cause
more competition for banks,

00:18:53.739 --> 00:18:56.367
which I think would be a very good thing.

00:18:56.367 --> 00:19:00.415
The other element of the payment
revolution that's going on globally is

00:19:00.415 --> 00:19:02.880
central bank digital currencies or CBDCs.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:10.984
And in this case, Alice and Bob simply
could have an account at the central bank.

00:19:10.984 --> 00:19:13.900
They would have an account at
the central bank in effect, and

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:17.216
Alice's message to pay Bob would
simply mean the dollars moves out of

00:19:17.216 --> 00:19:20.340
Alice's account at the central bank and
into Bob's account.

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:21.947
So that's very quick and easy.

00:19:21.947 --> 00:19:22.940
It would be super cheap.

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:29.132
It would in principle be available
to anyone anywhere on a simple

00:19:29.132 --> 00:19:33.195
mobile phone app or
on the Internet by any other device and

00:19:33.195 --> 00:19:37.955
make payments quite simple,
although there are some downsides.

00:19:37.955 --> 00:19:42.226
First of all, maybe you don't want the
central bank having to look after every

00:19:42.226 --> 00:19:46.840
single account in the United States, and
the Fed might be worried about that.

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:51.083
Secondly, the central bank is going to
have to check on the legality of all

00:19:51.083 --> 00:19:52.155
these payments.

00:19:52.155 --> 00:19:54.020
That's a big operation.

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:58.605
Moreover, in order to do that, central
bank needs to keep tabs on everyone and

00:19:58.605 --> 00:20:02.291
that's a potential concern for
those worried about privacy.

00:20:02.291 --> 00:20:06.915
So this trade off between privacy and
legal payments is

00:20:06.915 --> 00:20:12.680
causing a lot of concern in terms
of the design side of this.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:15.525
And I won't go into any details but
I hope you'll ask me about that later.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:21.662
I'll take one of the central banks
that's investigating this and

00:20:21.662 --> 00:20:24.650
just discuss what they
view as the benefits of or

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:28.470
objectives of exploring central
bank digital currencies.

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:34.756
So, one is making sure that
you're not relying on a flaky or

00:20:34.756 --> 00:20:40.435
sketchy payment provider,
so resilient payments.

00:20:40.435 --> 00:20:44.650
I'll skip to three, supporting
competition, efficiency, and innovation.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:48.221
Meeting future payment needs,
improving the availability and

00:20:48.221 --> 00:20:50.138
usability of central bank money,

00:20:50.138 --> 00:20:54.384
addressing the consequences of the decline
in paper cash which is an issue.

00:20:54.384 --> 00:20:57.409
What do you do once there's
no more paper money?

00:20:57.409 --> 00:20:59.595
I went to Sweden recently and

00:20:59.595 --> 00:21:04.524
they wouldn't take my paper
Swedish Krona to pay my hotel bill.

00:21:04.524 --> 00:21:08.554
And then provide a better method for
making cross border payments, better,

00:21:08.554 --> 00:21:09.611
faster, cheaper.

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:15.536
When we do see central bank
digital currencies in practice,

00:21:15.536 --> 00:21:20.001
they're mostly going to be done this way,
the way that China is doing it.

00:21:20.001 --> 00:21:24.934
So when Alice gets her central
bank digital currency in China

00:21:24.934 --> 00:21:29.577
which is going to be called
eCNY that means electronic CNY,

00:21:29.577 --> 00:21:34.328
CNY is the foreign exchange symbol for
Chinese renminbi.

00:21:34.328 --> 00:21:39.362
Alice will get it from a bank,
most likely her payment service provider,

00:21:39.362 --> 00:21:43.665
and she'll send a message to her
bank to tell the central bank to

00:21:43.665 --> 00:21:48.231
deduct 80 renminbi from her account,
put it in Bob's account.

00:21:48.231 --> 00:21:52.191
Bob will get a message from his payment
service provider that the money has been

00:21:52.191 --> 00:21:56.400
paid, and he'll give Alice the bread, and
that'll all happen in a second or two.

00:21:57.790 --> 00:22:02.069
And by the way, China's CBDC is
not done as a cryptocurrency or

00:22:02.069 --> 00:22:06.450
on a blockchain,
it's a central database management system.

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:11.072
And they're doing that in order to
get sufficient speed throughput of

00:22:11.072 --> 00:22:14.214
about 300,000 transactions a second.

00:22:14.214 --> 00:22:17.228
Although I think that cryptocurrency
experts will figure out how to do

00:22:17.228 --> 00:22:18.462
with that fast pretty soon.

00:22:21.105 --> 00:22:24.857
Now China doing this on its own,
as I mentioned, banks are helping,

00:22:24.857 --> 00:22:27.641
also telephone companies,
providing the apps and

00:22:27.641 --> 00:22:32.300
software, and FinTech payment service
providers, the traditional ones in China,

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:35.753
WeChat Pay and Alipay, and
many others in the private sector.

00:22:35.753 --> 00:22:39.676
And this is a likely public partnerships
in the United States as well,

00:22:39.676 --> 00:22:42.947
although in the US it's still
only at the research stage.

00:22:42.947 --> 00:22:46.537
The MIT and
the Federal Reserve Bank of Boston

00:22:46.537 --> 00:22:51.463
are collaborating on research led by Dr.
Neha Narula at MIT.

00:22:51.463 --> 00:22:54.860
But for now, it's just research,
there's no development.

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:59.016
Many other countries though, are
developing central bank due to currencies.

00:22:59.016 --> 00:23:05.488
You can see in this chart from the Bank
for International Settlements, significant

00:23:05.488 --> 00:23:12.320
increase in the last few years in the
number of central banks working on CBDCs.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:17.296
Some of them are only doing wholesale,
some are doing general purpose,

00:23:17.296 --> 00:23:18.774
most are doing both.

00:23:18.774 --> 00:23:23.270
And most of them are still
in the stage of experiments,

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:28.666
research, proof of concept, and
only a few are in development.

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:35.508
As I mentioned, most cases, you're
going to have this hybrid system where

00:23:35.508 --> 00:23:38.556
Alice doesn't deal directly
with the central bank,

00:23:38.556 --> 00:23:41.756
she deals directly with her
payment service provider.

00:23:41.756 --> 00:23:45.390
And you need to make sure that
the app that Alice gets for

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:50.288
her phone is completely interoperable
with the app that Bob is using, and

00:23:50.288 --> 00:23:53.141
that means very strong standard setting.

00:23:53.141 --> 00:23:58.622
So the design implications,
they take time to develop,

00:23:58.622 --> 00:24:04.278
and privacy The privacy side
of it securing the data

00:24:04.278 --> 00:24:10.214
securing the resiliency of the system
operationally against outages and

00:24:10.214 --> 00:24:17.200
also something to check on anti money
laundering and other illegal payments.

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:20.257
All of that needs to be designed
in a place like the United States.

00:24:22.966 --> 00:24:25.336
Here are some major CBDC developers.

00:24:25.336 --> 00:24:28.060
And as I mentioned,
China has already done it.

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:32.803
Officially the release comes next year,
but there are already hundreds of millions

00:24:32.803 --> 00:24:37.340
of people well, over 100 million
people in China have used it.

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:40.758
And for a large volume of payments,
Sweden has been doing this for

00:24:40.758 --> 00:24:42.920
development work for some time.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:47.333
Korea, Nigeria, Bank of Canada,
I'm a Canadian, by the way,

00:24:47.333 --> 00:24:51.320
Canada has said,
we're going to develop it now.

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:56.586
But we're not going to release it until
we feel that it's really necessary,

00:24:56.586 --> 00:25:02.096
and really necessary means maybe paper
money will not be any longer acceptable or

00:25:02.096 --> 00:25:07.038
maybe a private cryptocurrency that
we don't want to have in our payment

00:25:07.038 --> 00:25:12.090
system is getting too much traction so
we'll try to hit it off with a CBDC.

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:15.620
I've listed some readings at the bottom.

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:20.626
If you want to get in touch with me at
any time and ask questions on the side or

00:25:20.626 --> 00:25:24.430
ask for some of these readings,
let me know.

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:25.762
You can always reach me here
at the business school.

00:25:29.046 --> 00:25:31.410
I'm getting close to the end
of my prepared remarks.

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:34.360
So I hope you're getting
ready with your questions.

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:36.960
I'm looking forward to your comments and
your questions.

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:42.441
So what are the policy options and
let's say for the United States.

00:25:42.441 --> 00:25:47.470
By the way, one of my projects with Dr.
Elizabeth economy,

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:54.769
we're co-directing an expert group of 27
leading experts from national security,

00:25:54.769 --> 00:25:59.998
economics, finance, policy,
former government officials

00:25:59.998 --> 00:26:04.951
several former members of
the national security council.

00:26:04.951 --> 00:26:10.596
Former undersecretaries of the treasury,
former head of a central bank,

00:26:10.596 --> 00:26:16.510
some lot of top experts and our policy
group is attempting to provide at least,

00:26:16.510 --> 00:26:20.650
a roadmap for
policy to the United States government.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:26.160
And we're focusing in particular on
the implications of China's CBDC.

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:31.042
A lot of people are worried
that China may somehow take

00:26:31.042 --> 00:26:36.468
advantage of its central bank
digital currency to get ahead

00:26:36.468 --> 00:26:42.220
of the US in a way that the US
might need a plan to deal with.

00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:46.301
Among the plans are simply improve
the US payment system, but also provide

00:26:46.301 --> 00:26:50.838
a central bank digital currency that would
give the United States leading position

00:26:50.838 --> 00:26:55.440
internationally in how central bank
digital currencies are being used.

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:59.161
Then at this point, there's some
disagreement among the leadership at

00:26:59.161 --> 00:27:03.065
the Fed about whether a CBDC development
project would be a good idea or not,

00:27:03.065 --> 00:27:06.054
with some governors of the Fed saying,
there's no need,

00:27:06.054 --> 00:27:08.780
it's a solution in search of a problem.

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:12.930
And one central bank governor saying that
she can't get her head around the idea of

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:15.470
not developing a central
bank digital currency.

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:22.666
So US policy, well, one approach as I
mentioned, you could use regulation and

00:27:22.666 --> 00:27:27.536
fast payment infrastructure to get
a more open and competitive background

00:27:27.536 --> 00:27:32.017
payment system, something that
allows entry by Fintech firms, and

00:27:32.017 --> 00:27:36.346
basically reduces the walled garden
effect around the banks, and

00:27:36.346 --> 00:27:41.220
fast payments of course providing
the operational foundation.

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:46.227
The second approach is to allow or even
encourage compliant private stablecoins,

00:27:46.227 --> 00:27:49.874
subject of course,
to the regulations that you would need,

00:27:49.874 --> 00:27:52.879
including interoperability so
that people could

00:27:52.879 --> 00:27:57.190
exchange payments even if they didn't
have exactly the correct apps.

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:02.948
A third approach is to encourage
not necessarily stablecoins but

00:28:02.948 --> 00:28:08.800
other FinTech payments service providers
to enter the bank real payment systems.

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:12.070
And they could do that if they were to
get accounts at the Federal Reserve.

00:28:12.070 --> 00:28:16.220
Again, it would be subject to strong
compliance and interoperability standards.

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:20.185
And then finally the kind of the nuclear
option because it's such a big deal and

00:28:20.185 --> 00:28:23.072
it's so disruptive to banking and
the payment landscape

00:28:23.072 --> 00:28:27.260
is to introduce a general purpose,
central bank digital currency.

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:32.319
And when I testified to
the Senate Banking Committee this summer,

00:28:32.319 --> 00:28:37.557
I said you should develop in my view,
a central bank digital currency

00:28:37.557 --> 00:28:43.067
now should begin developing it now
because that takes five or six years at

00:28:43.067 --> 00:28:48.920
least before you'll have a CBDC that
you will know its costs and benefits.

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:51.386
And if you wait until you
think it's necessary,

00:28:51.386 --> 00:28:53.620
it'll take you five years from that point.

00:28:54.660 --> 00:28:59.851
But I'm hoping that option
one could be enough so

00:28:59.851 --> 00:29:06.010
that it doesn't have to be
run by the Central Bank.

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:10.124
My fear, however, that a CBDC may be
necessary and as I said, I'm recommending

00:29:10.124 --> 00:29:13.900
that United States pursue that
immediately rather than putting it off.

00:29:13.900 --> 00:29:17.926
The Fed is about to release a white
paper that's been delayed twice now,

00:29:17.926 --> 00:29:21.160
because I think it's still
making up its mind on whether or

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:23.150
at least what its stance should be.

00:29:23.150 --> 00:29:26.344
The white paper is only going to be
a discussion of costs and benefits and

00:29:26.344 --> 00:29:27.420
a request for comment.

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:35.328
But I think the Fed is genuinely uncertain
about whether it's a good idea and

00:29:35.328 --> 00:29:42.290
it's waiting for leadership from
Congress and the administration on this.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:47.900
So with that,
I want to turn it back to all of you.

00:29:47.900 --> 00:29:49.860
I feel like I've been
running a monologue here.

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:54.030
I want to hear what you have to say.

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:54.990
I see some chats.

00:29:56.520 --> 00:29:58.430
&gt;&gt; Yes,
we've had a lot of questions come in.

00:29:58.430 --> 00:30:03.128
Can we start off by sending a little
bit of baseline knowledge for

00:30:03.128 --> 00:30:07.217
our audience of what is
the difference between CBDCs and

00:30:07.217 --> 00:30:13.200
general cryptocurrency,
such as Bitcoin and how Blockchain works?

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:14.440
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:30:14.440 --> 00:30:19.540
Well, a CBDC doesn't tell you whether
it's a cryptocurrency meaning

00:30:19.540 --> 00:30:24.385
it's on a blockchain trades like
a tokenized money or whether it's

00:30:24.385 --> 00:30:30.270
as a central database management
system like the one that China has.

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:36.117
A cryptocurrency as you know is typically
an asset which is called the currency but

00:30:36.117 --> 00:30:40.014
in some cases like Bitcoin you
can call it a currency but

00:30:40.014 --> 00:30:43.120
it's an extremely volatile asset.

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:46.330
It's only used to some
extent to make payments.

00:30:46.330 --> 00:30:51.349
It's other uses are mostly
related to speculation.

00:30:54.973 --> 00:30:59.472
It has the advantage that it doesn't
require a trusted third party, and

00:30:59.472 --> 00:31:03.161
that's a very cool design,
and other cryptocurrencies,

00:31:03.161 --> 00:31:07.958
these are permissionless cryptocurrencies,
also have this advantage that

00:31:07.958 --> 00:31:12.828
they don't require a trusted third party,
everybody is democratically just

00:31:12.828 --> 00:31:17.730
transferring money on the same blockchain,
which is in the cloud somewhere.

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:25.407
A stablecoin is a type of cryptocurrency,
whose price is intended to be constant

00:31:25.407 --> 00:31:30.851
in terms of fiat money like one for
one and ideally, and right now stablecoins

00:31:30.851 --> 00:31:36.730
are being used mainly as a medium of
exchange in the cryptocurrency space.

00:31:36.730 --> 00:31:39.452
They're not yet being used much for

00:31:39.452 --> 00:31:44.090
general payments in the US retail
markets but they could be.

00:31:45.820 --> 00:31:49.317
So that's what I would say,
an answer to that question, but

00:31:49.317 --> 00:31:51.660
I'm happy to take a follow up.

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:54.001
&gt;&gt; Well, related to that,
we have somebody asked,

00:31:54.001 --> 00:31:58.240
could you comment on the valuation
approach for non-stable cryptocurrencies?

00:31:58.240 --> 00:31:59.650
Like Bitcoin.

00:31:59.650 --> 00:32:01.809
&gt;&gt; Okay, so it's a bit like gold.

00:32:01.809 --> 00:32:06.171
In fact, Larry Summers recently said it's
the new gold in the sense that it's worth

00:32:06.171 --> 00:32:08.397
whatever people are willing to pay for it.

00:32:08.397 --> 00:32:12.590
Hoping that they can sell it later to
somebody else who will pay more for it.

00:32:12.590 --> 00:32:17.220
And I think that characterizes
most cryptocurrency investments.

00:32:17.220 --> 00:32:20.689
And they are getting very popular.

00:32:20.689 --> 00:32:24.524
I read a report yesterday by one
of Stanford's former PhD students,

00:32:24.524 --> 00:32:28.042
Michael Schwartz,
who's the chief economist at Microsoft.

00:32:28.042 --> 00:32:33.226
Who feels that there's a tremendous
amount of potential growth in the total

00:32:33.226 --> 00:32:37.607
market value of cryptocurrencies,
not only for speculation.

00:32:37.607 --> 00:32:41.819
But because people want a place
to store money that they don't

00:32:41.819 --> 00:32:44.350
want anyone else to know about.

00:32:44.350 --> 00:32:47.799
So right now they're using
paper $100 bills, or

00:32:47.799 --> 00:32:51.718
paper 500 euro notes, or
1,000 swiss franc notes.

00:32:51.718 --> 00:32:55.035
And that's pretty cumbersome,
and it's hard to transfer those.

00:32:55.035 --> 00:32:59.025
But it's working pretty well for the
people that want to keep their wealth in

00:32:59.025 --> 00:33:02.269
a private forum, and
that would include a lot of criminals.

00:33:02.269 --> 00:33:07.697
But other than storing wealth privately,
which is a bit illicit

00:33:07.697 --> 00:33:12.835
in many cases or very illicit,
or making illegal payments.

00:33:12.835 --> 00:33:17.580
Bitcoin and many other currencies
are used for speculative purposes.

00:33:17.580 --> 00:33:20.360
And I think that's the majority
of the transactions are.

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:22.401
People hoping Bitcoin will go up and
they buy it, or

00:33:22.401 --> 00:33:24.350
they think it might be going down so
they sell it.

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:26.275
It's extremely volatile.

00:33:26.275 --> 00:33:31.601
As far as as valuing it, as I said,
if you can put a price on gold,

00:33:31.601 --> 00:33:35.580
then you can put a price
on Bitcoin pretty well.

00:33:37.410 --> 00:33:42.198
&gt;&gt; What are the implications of
all of this on money laundering?

00:33:42.198 --> 00:33:42.753
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
And

00:33:42.753 --> 00:33:46.570
let me couple that with a question I see,
is Bitcoin really private?

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:51.471
And so let's couple that with the issue
of money laundering or ransom payments,

00:33:51.471 --> 00:33:55.376
which happened recently when
the US oil pipeline was shut down.

00:33:55.376 --> 00:34:02.140
And in demand by a cyber attack,
and the ransom was paid in Bitcoin.

00:34:02.140 --> 00:34:09.850
So money laundering, ransom payments and
privacy, well, it's not private at all.

00:34:09.850 --> 00:34:12.282
Everybody can see the blockchain,

00:34:12.282 --> 00:34:16.980
everybody has full vision of
Bitcoin on the blockchain.

00:34:16.980 --> 00:34:20.810
But of course,
you don't have the name of the person,

00:34:20.810 --> 00:34:24.823
like it doesn't say
Darrell Duffie has 212 Bitcoin.

00:34:24.823 --> 00:34:28.111
It just gives the ISP, and so

00:34:28.111 --> 00:34:33.520
people can track what I'm doing by that.

00:34:33.520 --> 00:34:38.191
It turns out that the US authorities are
starting to get pretty good at tracking

00:34:38.191 --> 00:34:40.618
payments of Bitcoin on the blockchain.

00:34:40.618 --> 00:34:46.271
And they actually tracked down
the ransom payments and told the,

00:34:46.271 --> 00:34:52.040
well, basically got back several
million dollars of the ransom.

00:34:52.040 --> 00:34:58.032
And more recently, a lot of Bitcoin
was stolen from the blockchain,

00:34:58.032 --> 00:35:04.350
and the thieves were forced to give
it back because they were called out.

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:08.781
And everybody knew where those
bitcoins were going in effect, and

00:35:08.781 --> 00:35:12.750
were tracking them, so
they basically get most of it back.

00:35:12.750 --> 00:35:13.703
So it's not private.

00:35:13.703 --> 00:35:17.272
Paper money, if you really want to hide
your wealth is much more effective,

00:35:17.272 --> 00:35:20.558
except that you have to then guard
it in a much more expensive way, and

00:35:20.558 --> 00:35:22.329
it's hard to move around the world.

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:26.600
&gt;&gt; What would back the US stable coin?

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:31.477
And what is the estimated cost
reduction from the 2% of GDP?

00:35:31.477 --> 00:35:34.811
&gt;&gt; Okay, well,
let's talk about the backing,

00:35:34.811 --> 00:35:38.460
it's the same backing as paper money.

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:42.005
So when the Central Bank
issues paper money,

00:35:42.005 --> 00:35:46.020
there is no backing,
it's just an I owe you.

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:50.664
So it says the Fed owes you if you
have $100 bill, the Fed owes you $100.

00:35:50.664 --> 00:35:53.180
So you could use that $100.

00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:57.063
If you got enough dollars, you could use
it to buy from the Fed Treasury securities

00:35:57.063 --> 00:35:59.100
through your bank or something like that.

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:03.885
But there's nothing in the sense
that there's no money in the, or

00:36:03.885 --> 00:36:07.090
there's no gold in a vault or
physical assets backing it.

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:08.070
It's an I owe you.

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:12.603
Just like when you have money in your
bank, it's not, there's no stack of coins

00:36:12.603 --> 00:36:16.820
or anything else sitting in the bank
waiting for you to come and collect it.

00:36:16.820 --> 00:36:21.376
It's simply an I owe you, and when you
call the I owe you, the bank says, okay,

00:36:21.376 --> 00:36:24.790
we'll pay you and
we'll cash in something else to do that.

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:31.209
So almost all money in the world now is
in the form of an I owe you, one way or

00:36:31.209 --> 00:36:31.889
another.

00:36:31.889 --> 00:36:34.009
And almost none of it has backing.

00:36:34.009 --> 00:36:38.715
Many years ago, fiat currencies like
the dollar and most currencies in fact,

00:36:38.715 --> 00:36:40.570
had gold backing.

00:36:40.570 --> 00:36:44.769
But that was abandoned, the supply of
gold became too limited and in any case,

00:36:44.769 --> 00:36:47.880
the price of gold is too
volatile to be a backing.

00:36:47.880 --> 00:36:52.128
The monetary authorities are able
to keep the price of fiat currency,

00:36:52.128 --> 00:36:53.429
reasonably stable.

00:36:53.429 --> 00:36:58.870
US inflation has been between zero and
2% for about three decades.

00:36:58.870 --> 00:37:01.520
So it's with some ups and downs.

00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:06.007
Right now it's a bit more than two, but
fiat currencies are very stable in price.

00:37:06.007 --> 00:37:08.770
So you don't need to worry about
the backing for the dollar.

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:10.917
Maybe if you were in Zimbabwe or

00:37:10.917 --> 00:37:15.632
some other country who has a history
of very, very high inflation.

00:37:15.632 --> 00:37:20.568
You might be worried that there's nothing
backing your paper money so maybe you

00:37:20.568 --> 00:37:25.225
should trade it for something else like
a different fiat currency or gold.

00:37:25.225 --> 00:37:27.328
&gt;&gt; Hi, this is Zack from Singapore.

00:37:27.328 --> 00:37:32.722
So I want to really ask you the question
about the regulator and the regulation.

00:37:32.722 --> 00:37:35.172
Because recently it
sounds like government,

00:37:35.172 --> 00:37:38.565
Swiss language launched before and
the Singapore government,

00:37:38.565 --> 00:37:42.478
also the Central Bank had just launched
a digital payment token license.

00:37:42.478 --> 00:37:47.790
Also tried to address some of the AML and
anti money laundering issue here.

00:37:47.790 --> 00:37:49.330
So what's your view on the regulator?

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:55.570
What's the regulator role here for the
regulating all those different currency?

00:37:55.570 --> 00:38:01.490
&gt;&gt; The regulator really should be running
the show because money is a public good.

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:03.830
It's like the Internet.

00:38:03.830 --> 00:38:06.955
It's like the Internet,
it's like the highway system,

00:38:06.955 --> 00:38:10.293
the air traffic control system,
these are all public goods.

00:38:10.293 --> 00:38:13.881
They're utilities that we all
depend on and that any one firm or

00:38:13.881 --> 00:38:18.349
individual doesn't really internalize
all the benefits of keeping money and

00:38:18.349 --> 00:38:20.056
the payments system stable.

00:38:20.056 --> 00:38:21.229
And highly efficient.

00:38:21.229 --> 00:38:25.940
So the government really or regulators
really have a key role in deciding on

00:38:25.940 --> 00:38:30.100
the design of the payment system,
and how to regulate its use.

00:38:30.100 --> 00:38:34.057
Now that said,
the private sector such as yourself, Zack,

00:38:34.057 --> 00:38:39.740
you're the entrepreneurs that are working
within the rules that the regulator sets.

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:43.060
I know Ravi Menon at
the Monetary Authority of Singapore.

00:38:43.060 --> 00:38:47.936
He's an excellent regulator and I'm sure
he and the Monetary Authority of Singapore

00:38:47.936 --> 00:38:50.992
are very effective at working
with firms like yours.

00:38:50.992 --> 00:38:55.852
Singapore, one of the most advanced
treatments of FinTech payments of

00:38:55.852 --> 00:38:57.640
any country in the world.

00:38:57.640 --> 00:38:59.870
It's way ahead of many other countries.

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:05.000
As you know, you have your fast payment
system which is very effective.

00:39:05.000 --> 00:39:08.176
And Singapore has a cross
border fast payment system now.

00:39:08.176 --> 00:39:12.931
And moreover, Singapore is considering
ahead of most countries what, and

00:39:12.931 --> 00:39:16.807
carefully what to do about
Central Bank digital currencies.

00:39:16.807 --> 00:39:19.520
And I've seen some of their work on that.

00:39:19.520 --> 00:39:24.258
So you can be, I think very
confident that your country is

00:39:24.258 --> 00:39:28.516
doing a great job from
the regulatory standpoint.

00:39:28.516 --> 00:39:31.457
And making sure that citizens
in Singapore have access to

00:39:31.457 --> 00:39:33.300
efficient payments that are safe.

00:39:34.780 --> 00:39:39.466
&gt;&gt; So my question tags on to
the last month, and that is,

00:39:39.466 --> 00:39:42.488
what are your recommendations for

00:39:42.488 --> 00:39:47.830
keeping the regulatory bodies
relatively unpoliticized?

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:53.110
because now what we're talking about is
who regulates and what's their agenda.

00:39:53.110 --> 00:39:56.240
So, because we're talking
about things that have.

00:39:56.240 --> 00:40:01.122
Massive global implications
as opposed to the typical

00:40:01.122 --> 00:40:04.950
geographically centered fiat currency.

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:08.701
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Bernard,
I totally agree with your comment.

00:40:08.701 --> 00:40:12.993
You want the regulators to be
non-political and also aligned, so

00:40:12.993 --> 00:40:14.608
they need to have a plan.

00:40:14.608 --> 00:40:17.346
And that plan is first up to Congress,

00:40:17.346 --> 00:40:21.550
which authorizes the administration
to design the plan.

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:24.243
And the United States is behind,
and Congress and

00:40:24.243 --> 00:40:26.820
the administration have
agreed we are behind.

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:28.395
They're very explicit.

00:40:28.395 --> 00:40:33.769
They have said, we should have already
known what to do about stablecoins and

00:40:33.769 --> 00:40:35.675
cryptocurrencies.

00:40:35.675 --> 00:40:38.618
But now we're catching up,
that's basically what's happening.

00:40:38.618 --> 00:40:42.323
And you have a little bit of,
it's not just the regulator in the US,

00:40:42.323 --> 00:40:44.115
it's many regulators.

00:40:44.115 --> 00:40:49.495
So the Securities and Exchange Commission
under Chair Gary Gensler is taking

00:40:49.495 --> 00:40:55.140
a lot of action under the authorities
of the securities regulations.

00:40:55.140 --> 00:41:03.560
Other regulators are taking a bit
more time to be thoughtful about it.

00:41:03.560 --> 00:41:05.652
The Fed is, in my view, being so

00:41:05.652 --> 00:41:09.384
thoughtful that they're
falling behind even further.

00:41:09.384 --> 00:41:11.210
And they need to move along.

00:41:11.210 --> 00:41:15.057
I've had notes from people in Congress,
I won't name names,

00:41:15.057 --> 00:41:19.830
that have suggested that the Fed
should be moving along faster on this.

00:41:19.830 --> 00:41:24.764
And Chair Powell of the Fed has said he's
genuinely uncertain whether the US needs

00:41:24.764 --> 00:41:26.849
a central bank digital currency.

00:41:26.849 --> 00:41:27.843
And as I mentioned,

00:41:27.843 --> 00:41:31.834
several governors of the Fed are kind of
pointing different directions on this.

00:41:31.834 --> 00:41:34.080
So the Fed needs to move along as well.

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:37.880
But I think the Fed is waiting for
guidance from the administration.

00:41:37.880 --> 00:41:42.444
So I think President Biden's
administration will be providing guidance

00:41:42.444 --> 00:41:44.970
on this issue in the near future.

00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:46.097
And then the Fed will move.

00:41:46.097 --> 00:41:50.280
It will release its white paper,
get comments, and decide what to do.

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:55.090
Will they allow stablecoins, don't know.

00:41:55.090 --> 00:41:58.060
Will they promote development of CBDC,
don't know.

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:03.057
My suspicion is, though, that they will
try to do better with the bank rail

00:42:03.057 --> 00:42:06.230
payment system and
maybe they will make the case.

00:42:06.230 --> 00:42:08.025
We don't need all that new stuff.

00:42:08.025 --> 00:42:11.897
What we need to do is
improve the old stuff.

00:42:11.897 --> 00:42:16.512
Put in fast bank rail payments,
do a better job on

00:42:16.512 --> 00:42:21.813
competition with banking,
and let the banks run this.

00:42:21.813 --> 00:42:26.050
They have done a reasonable
job on anti-money laundering.

00:42:26.050 --> 00:42:29.700
They've done a reasonable job
on protecting your privacy.

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:34.952
In my view, they've done an unreasonably
bad job on competing with each other to

00:42:34.952 --> 00:42:41.060
give you good services at low prices, and
to innovate with smarter payment systems.

00:42:41.060 --> 00:42:46.520
And as a result, here we are today,
way behind in the United States.

00:42:46.520 --> 00:42:49.516
Every economist and every regulator
that I've spoken to agrees that

00:42:49.516 --> 00:42:52.940
the United States is further behind than
any other major country in this area.

00:42:56.084 --> 00:42:59.170
&gt;&gt; Hi, so it's Mark Louis.

00:42:59.170 --> 00:43:00.123
And Professor Duffy,

00:43:00.123 --> 00:43:03.240
I will confess this is an area that I'm
still trying to get my head around.

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:06.382
So I may have some fundamental
misunderstandings of how some of

00:43:06.382 --> 00:43:07.730
this works.

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:12.416
But to me, a central bank digital currency
is just an electronic form of another fiat

00:43:12.416 --> 00:43:14.404
currency, like a paper currency.

00:43:14.404 --> 00:43:19.075
And so in my mind, notwithstanding the
fact that there's a lot of design issues

00:43:19.075 --> 00:43:22.352
and regulatory issues and
whatnot, it seems to me, and

00:43:22.352 --> 00:43:27.094
this is an editorial comment from somebody
that's not very well informed, to be

00:43:27.094 --> 00:43:32.010
the natural extension of what the Fed does
in terms of the paper money in the system.

00:43:33.670 --> 00:43:38.112
So I find it attractive, or at least
interesting from that perspective.

00:43:38.112 --> 00:43:42.056
The issue probably is what it does
to the existing commercial banks,

00:43:42.056 --> 00:43:44.210
both in the US and elsewhere.

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:49.837
Because as a consumer or as a businessman,
I could see that if I was able to transact

00:43:49.837 --> 00:43:54.813
at the Central Bank, as much fun as
the guys at Chase and Wells Fargo are,

00:43:54.813 --> 00:44:00.480
they would become disintermediated
in probably less than ten seconds.

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:03.316
And so I guess the question
that I've got for you, and

00:44:03.316 --> 00:44:05.837
I'm not really sure it's
the right question,

00:44:05.837 --> 00:44:09.760
is what does this do to the commercial
bank infrastructure, if you will?

00:44:11.200 --> 00:44:16.928
What does it do to the profit model of a
commercial bank, to their balance sheets?

00:44:16.928 --> 00:44:21.606
And there are other values the commercial
banks are having the economy

00:44:21.606 --> 00:44:25.885
in terms of conducting monetary policy,
and multipliers, and

00:44:25.885 --> 00:44:28.340
all sorts of other things.

00:44:28.340 --> 00:44:30.112
So it seems like I'm fumbling.

00:44:30.112 --> 00:44:34.187
But it seems like such a radical
reordering of the financial system,

00:44:34.187 --> 00:44:37.520
that I just have trouble
getting my head around it.

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:39.824
Anyway, I'm not sure where
the question is in there, but

00:44:39.824 --> 00:44:41.510
just I'd love to hear your comments.

00:44:41.510 --> 00:44:43.960
&gt;&gt; Well, Mark, you bet, you've nailed it.

00:44:43.960 --> 00:44:47.994
That's the $64 trillion
question that you've nailed.

00:44:47.994 --> 00:44:50.773
We have a weak payment
system because banks don't

00:44:50.773 --> 00:44:52.698
have incentive to make it better.

00:44:52.698 --> 00:44:56.962
And if the alternative is everything
is done at the central bank,

00:44:56.962 --> 00:44:59.764
that disrupts the entire banking system.

00:44:59.764 --> 00:45:04.598
And so you've got these two extremes, both
of which are not that attractive actually.

00:45:04.598 --> 00:45:09.584
Because, I mean, central banks are very
effective at what they do, but they're

00:45:09.584 --> 00:45:14.447
not noted for facing every single citizen
in the country with retail services.

00:45:14.447 --> 00:45:15.982
And they might mess that up.

00:45:15.982 --> 00:45:18.320
They might mess up your data security.

00:45:19.390 --> 00:45:24.881
They might have an operational outage and,
whoops, nobody can pay anybody.

00:45:24.881 --> 00:45:28.002
So we don't want to go whole hog,
all the way to the extreme.

00:45:28.002 --> 00:45:31.406
And if you go the other way,
of course, if you just say,

00:45:31.406 --> 00:45:36.730
well, we don't want to do that, we'll
just let the banks continue as they are.

00:45:36.730 --> 00:45:39.237
That's also terrible because,
as I mentioned,

00:45:39.237 --> 00:45:41.756
we have a decrepit payment system,
if you ask me.

00:45:41.756 --> 00:45:44.190
And I think most people
would agree with that.

00:45:44.190 --> 00:45:46.008
So what's the happy medium?

00:45:46.008 --> 00:45:51.575
The happy medium is either regulate
the banks harder for competition,

00:45:51.575 --> 00:45:56.770
allow entry by FinTech firms, or
force banks to compete harder and

00:45:56.770 --> 00:45:59.940
open up banking services at lower cost.

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:06.280
Or introduce a central bank digital
currency that's not the extreme version

00:46:06.280 --> 00:46:07.470
that I just described.

00:46:07.470 --> 00:46:09.895
It's a version in which the banks and

00:46:09.895 --> 00:46:13.280
other payment service
providers are facing you.

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:17.108
They are designing the software
that you are using on

00:46:17.108 --> 00:46:22.330
your mobile phone up to a uniform
standard of interoperability.

00:46:22.330 --> 00:46:26.019
And they are responsible for
monitoring your payments for legality and

00:46:26.019 --> 00:46:30.480
protecting your data, just as we
have today in terms of monitoring.

00:46:30.480 --> 00:46:35.652
So I would guess that the US
government is going to figure this out.

00:46:35.652 --> 00:46:38.320
And they're going to take
one of those two approaches.

00:46:38.320 --> 00:46:41.363
A, fix the current bank
rail payment system, or B,

00:46:41.363 --> 00:46:45.928
introduced a hybrid system in which
the banks are still running everything but

00:46:45.928 --> 00:46:48.610
you're using central bank money.

00:46:48.610 --> 00:46:52.943
And going back to your question, Mark,
even that will be disruptive to the banks.

00:46:52.943 --> 00:46:55.298
Because if you're using
central bank money a lot,

00:46:55.298 --> 00:46:59.240
that means you're not using your
bank deposits to make payments.

00:46:59.240 --> 00:47:00.735
Banks will have to go out and

00:47:00.735 --> 00:47:04.641
get their funding on the wholesale
funding market to a greater extent.

00:47:04.641 --> 00:47:08.930
The cost of their funding will go up,
bank profitability will go down.

00:47:08.930 --> 00:47:10.360
That's a commonly agreed thing.

00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:15.223
And then we need to think about,
will that affect loan provision?

00:47:15.223 --> 00:47:17.893
Will banks then not want
to make as many loans,

00:47:17.893 --> 00:47:20.840
because now their deposit
funding is more costly?

00:47:20.840 --> 00:47:24.207
And there's a massive debate
going on in that area as well.

00:47:24.207 --> 00:47:28.513
Where there are some that say, well,
banks will become less profitable, but

00:47:28.513 --> 00:47:31.670
they weren't offering any loans at a loss.

00:47:31.670 --> 00:47:35.190
So they're going to continue making the
same loans that they were already making.

00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:36.810
And others say, well, you don't get it.

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:39.347
I mean, I'm one of the former camp.

00:47:39.347 --> 00:47:41.524
Others will say, you don't get it.

00:47:41.524 --> 00:47:45.277
If you cause banks to have to pay more for
deposits and

00:47:45.277 --> 00:47:49.645
compete harder,
they'll be more reluctant to make loans.

00:47:49.645 --> 00:47:54.800
So that all has to be sorted, and
it's no wonder the Fed is having trouble

00:47:54.800 --> 00:47:59.477
Making up its mind but it's going to have
to make up its mind pretty soon because

00:47:59.477 --> 00:48:03.076
not only is it neat,
is there a benefit to making a choice.

00:48:03.076 --> 00:48:07.638
Everybody now is commenting and the Fed
is under pressure the government and

00:48:07.638 --> 00:48:10.703
therefore the administration
is under pressure to

00:48:10.703 --> 00:48:14.150
resolve what's the best course to take.

00:48:14.150 --> 00:48:16.802
And this is all changed if you
asked someone five years ago and

00:48:16.802 --> 00:48:18.730
the government should we introduce a CBDC?

00:48:18.730 --> 00:48:20.439
They would say, well, not really,

00:48:20.439 --> 00:48:23.500
because everything is there's
nobody really needs that.

00:48:23.500 --> 00:48:27.210
Now everybody on the government is saying,
gosh, that's a really tough question.

00:48:27.210 --> 00:48:32.019
I don't know but
we should try to solve that right away.

00:48:32.019 --> 00:48:33.732
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Hi, Professor Duffy,

00:48:33.732 --> 00:48:37.715
my question follows on Mark's question
a bit of the big picture macro

00:48:37.715 --> 00:48:41.870
economic effects of having
a central bank digital currency.

00:48:41.870 --> 00:48:47.605
And I'm wondering, from your view,
what are all of the other areas where

00:48:47.605 --> 00:48:53.545
having a almost instantaneous transfer
of money is going to be disruptive.

00:48:53.545 --> 00:48:59.120
Because for example, in the securities
markets we have T+2 settlement right now.

00:48:59.120 --> 00:49:04.016
Lots of security fails and of course that
just moderates the velocity of money

00:49:04.016 --> 00:49:09.008
by having this two day settlement inside
of the securities markets that I'm.

00:49:09.008 --> 00:49:13.482
And you think about how households have
their paychecks going into the bank, so

00:49:13.482 --> 00:49:17.810
then that also mitigates sort of how
fast they can be transferring money.

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:22.972
So what are your views on having
a reasonably constructed central bank

00:49:22.972 --> 00:49:27.883
card currency as you had said,
either through banks or directly.

00:49:27.883 --> 00:49:34.930
What would macro economists need to factor
in that is a new world in that situation?

00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:37.231
&gt;&gt; Well, that's a super good question and

00:49:37.231 --> 00:49:41.250
it's another one that's fostered
a huge amount of debate.

00:49:41.250 --> 00:49:44.932
So, it sounds like you might have
been working on Wall Street or

00:49:44.932 --> 00:49:47.240
you might be working on Wall Street.

00:49:47.240 --> 00:49:48.200
&gt;&gt; It's a regulator.

00:49:48.200 --> 00:49:49.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah, okay.

00:49:49.450 --> 00:49:53.621
Well then you know the famous saying on
Wall Street, that nothing good happens

00:49:53.621 --> 00:49:57.680
between the time at which a trade is
made and the time at which it's settled.

00:49:58.710 --> 00:50:03.372
In other words, if you can, settle
trades faster because then you're not

00:50:03.372 --> 00:50:08.108
exposed to counter party risk and moreover
the money that you have tied up and

00:50:08.108 --> 00:50:12.262
the securities that you have tied
up until the settlement is done.

00:50:12.262 --> 00:50:17.178
Or trapped or committed and they're in
you want to get those released as soon as

00:50:17.178 --> 00:50:21.520
possible so that the system
